---
/**
 * Notebook — Page Archetype
 *
 * Short-form notes, bookmarks, links, observations. The informal content type.
 * Always includes: compact entries with mono timestamps, minimal chrome, consistent entry structure.
 * Never includes: card-based layouts, masonry grids, thumbnail images.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Notes" description="Short observations, bookmarks, and references.">
  <div class="pt-[var(--space-9)] content-reading">
    <h1 class="text-display font-display text-[var(--color-fg)]">Notes</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)]">
      Short observations, bookmarks, and references.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-12 content-reading space-y-0">
    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-09-22">Sep 22, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">color</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        The <a href="https://git.apcacontrast.com/documentation/APCA_in_a_Nutshell.html" class="underline underline-offset-[0.2em] decoration-1 text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:text-[var(--color-accent-hover)] transition-colors duration-[var(--motion-fast)] ease-remarque">APCA documentation</a> describes its Accessible Perceptual Contrast Algorithm as “the candidate contrast method for the future WCAG 3.” Remarque treats it as advisory data, not a gate: WCAG 2.x ratios remain the pass/fail check, and the audit prints APCA Lc beside each ratio as report-only information (REMARQUE.md, “APCA: Considered, Not Adopted”).
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-08-11">Aug 11, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">typography</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        Butterick’s <a href="https://practicaltypography.com/line-length.html" class="underline underline-offset-[0.2em] decoration-1 text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:text-[var(--color-accent-hover)] transition-colors duration-[var(--motion-fast)] ease-remarque">line length</a> chapter recommends: “Aim for an average line length of 45–90 characters, including spaces.” Bringhurst’s 45–75 (§2.1.2) is the stricter range; Remarque’s 38rem column lands inside both.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-07-07">Jul 7, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">accessibility</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        WCAG 2.2 <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/text-spacing.html" class="underline underline-offset-[0.2em] decoration-1 text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:text-[var(--color-accent-hover)] transition-colors duration-[var(--motion-fast)] ease-remarque">SC 1.4.12 Text Spacing</a> (Level AA) requires that nothing is lost when a reader overrides line height to at least 1.5 times the font size, paragraph spacing to 2 times, letter spacing to 0.12 times and word spacing to 0.16 times. Unitless line heights and relative units make that pass by default; Remarque’s reflow suite tests it.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-06-09">Jun 9, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">accessibility</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        WCAG 2.2 <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum.html" class="underline underline-offset-[0.2em] decoration-1 text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:text-[var(--color-accent-hover)] transition-colors duration-[var(--motion-fast)] ease-remarque">SC 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum)</a> is Level AA and asks for pointer targets of at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels, with exceptions such as spacing, inline links and essential cases. Remarque sets 44px for interactive elements, which clears it with margin.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-05-12">May 12, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">css</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        MDN’s <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/color_value/light-dark" class="underline underline-offset-[0.2em] decoration-1 text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:text-[var(--color-accent-hover)] transition-colors duration-[var(--motion-fast)] ease-remarque">light-dark()</a> page: the function takes two colors and returns one based on the active color scheme, with no <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] bg-[var(--color-code-bg)] text-[var(--color-code-fg)] px-[0.35em] py-[0.15em] rounded-[var(--radius-sm)]">prefers-color-scheme</code> media query. It only switches when <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] bg-[var(--color-code-bg)] text-[var(--color-code-fg)] px-[0.35em] py-[0.15em] rounded-[var(--radius-sm)]">color-scheme</code> allows both schemes, usually <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] bg-[var(--color-code-bg)] text-[var(--color-code-fg)] px-[0.35em] py-[0.15em] rounded-[var(--radius-sm)]">light dark</code> on <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] bg-[var(--color-code-bg)] text-[var(--color-code-fg)] px-[0.35em] py-[0.15em] rounded-[var(--radius-sm)]">:root</code>. It has worked across current browsers since May 2024, and every Remarque color token is one <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] bg-[var(--color-code-bg)] text-[var(--color-code-fg)] px-[0.35em] py-[0.15em] rounded-[var(--radius-sm)]">light-dark()</code> declaration.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-04-17">Apr 17, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">color</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        Björn Ottosson’s <a href="https://bottosson.github.io/posts/oklab/" class="underline underline-offset-[0.2em] decoration-1 text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:text-[var(--color-accent-hover)] transition-colors duration-[var(--motion-fast)] ease-remarque">A perceptual color space for image processing</a> is the original Oklab write-up, the space behind OKLCH. Its stated goal is that Oklab “should predict lightness, chroma and hue well.” The post dates from December 2020; CSS Color Level 4 later added <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] bg-[var(--color-code-bg)] text-[var(--color-code-fg)] px-[0.35em] py-[0.15em] rounded-[var(--radius-sm)]">oklab()</code> and <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] bg-[var(--color-code-bg)] text-[var(--color-code-fg)] px-[0.35em] py-[0.15em] rounded-[var(--radius-sm)]">oklch()</code>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-04-10">Apr 10, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">typography</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        Matthew Butterick’s <a href="https://practicaltypography.com/what-is-typography.html" class="underline underline-offset-[0.2em] decoration-1 text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:text-[var(--color-accent-hover)] transition-colors duration-[var(--motion-fast)] ease-remarque">Practical Typography</a> is a free online book on typography for people who create documents. It opens with a definition: “Typography is the visual component of the written word.” That framing, typography as part of the language rather than a layer applied to it, is the foundation Remarque builds on.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-04-07">Apr 7, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">design-systems</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        The problem with most design systems is that they optimize for the builder, not the reader. A comprehensive component library is a productivity tool for developers. A typographic system is a readability tool for humans. These are different goals with different tradeoffs.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-04-03">Apr 3, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">css</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        OKLCH is a CSS color notation built on Oklab, which is designed so lightness, chroma and hue can be adjusted with far more predictable results than in HSL. Equal lightness across hues looks roughly equally light, so there is less manual eyeballing of “does this blue look as bright as that green?”
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-03-28">Mar 28, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">reference</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        Robert Bringhurst gives 45–75 characters per line as a satisfactory range for a single-column page (<em>The Elements of Typographic Style</em>, §2.1.2); Butterick allows 45–90. At 17px Inter, <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] bg-[var(--color-code-bg)] text-[var(--color-code-fg)] px-[0.35em] py-[0.15em] rounded-[var(--radius-sm)]">38rem</code> sets about 71 characters — measured, not computed: the <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] bg-[var(--color-code-bg)] text-[var(--color-code-fg)] px-[0.35em] py-[0.15em] rounded-[var(--radius-sm)]">ch</code> unit is the width of “0” and overstates how few characters fit.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="py-6 border-b border-[var(--color-border)]">
      <div class="font-mono text-[length:var(--text-meta)] tracking-[var(--tracking-meta)] text-[var(--color-muted)] mb-2">
        <time datetime="2026-03-25">Mar 25, 2026</time>
        <span class="ml-3">tools</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[length:var(--text-body)] text-[var(--color-fg)]">
        Newsreader by Production Type is an underappreciated typeface for the web. Variable weight, variable optical size, open source. It has the authority of a traditional serif without the heaviness of Georgia or the overuse of Playfair Display.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
